WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:09.880 --> 00:00:10.868
[CROWD CHATTER]

00:00:10.868 --> 00:00:12.350
KRISTEN RAILEY: Girls who--

00:00:12.350 --> 00:00:13.360
ALL: Build.

00:00:13.360 --> 00:00:15.569
KRISTEN RAILEY: OK,
that wasn't loud enough.

00:00:15.569 --> 00:00:16.360
Let's try it again.

00:00:16.360 --> 00:00:17.680
Go ahead and talk.

00:00:17.680 --> 00:00:19.050
Ignore me.

00:00:19.050 --> 00:00:21.050
[CROWD CHATTER]

00:00:21.050 --> 00:00:22.050
Girls who--

00:00:22.050 --> 00:00:22.910
ALL: Build.

00:00:22.910 --> 00:00:23.910
KRISTEN RAILEY: Awesome.

00:00:23.910 --> 00:00:25.510
Good morning, you guys.

00:00:25.510 --> 00:00:26.700
My name's Kristen.

00:00:26.700 --> 00:00:29.400
You've probably gotten
one or two emails from me.

00:00:29.400 --> 00:00:31.180
Thank you all for being here.

00:00:31.180 --> 00:00:34.540
The volunteers and I-- they're
wearing these awesome navy

00:00:34.540 --> 00:00:37.160
T-shirts-- we're really
excited to teach you

00:00:37.160 --> 00:00:39.430
guys engineering concepts
through something

00:00:39.430 --> 00:00:41.860
you use every day, cameras.

00:00:41.860 --> 00:00:45.670
How many of you guys
have a camera phone?

00:00:45.670 --> 00:00:47.100
Raise them higher.

00:00:47.100 --> 00:00:47.870
OK.

00:00:47.870 --> 00:00:49.260
Mostly everyone.

00:00:49.260 --> 00:00:53.030
How about, does
anyone have Instagram?

00:00:53.030 --> 00:00:54.990
Snapchat?

00:00:54.990 --> 00:00:56.150
Nice.

00:00:56.150 --> 00:00:59.530
So I love those things
too, but I am also

00:00:59.530 --> 00:01:02.610
an engineer, which is why
I started this program.

00:01:02.610 --> 00:01:04.860
Cameras are this
great integration

00:01:04.860 --> 00:01:07.620
of all these different
technologies, computer science,

00:01:07.620 --> 00:01:09.860
mechanical engineering,
all sorts of things,

00:01:09.860 --> 00:01:12.710
and you guys interact
with them every day.

00:01:12.710 --> 00:01:15.170
So it looks like you guys
have gotten your hands on them

00:01:15.170 --> 00:01:17.420
already and taken them apart.

00:01:17.420 --> 00:01:21.360
Did anything surprise
you on what you found?

00:01:21.360 --> 00:01:23.090
You can talk to the
people next to you.

00:01:26.066 --> 00:01:29.538
[CROWD CHATTER]

00:01:31.522 --> 00:01:33.010
Girls who--

00:01:33.010 --> 00:01:34.256
ALL: Build.

00:01:34.256 --> 00:01:36.380
KRISTEN RAILEY: OK, how
about this group over here.

00:01:36.380 --> 00:01:38.793
Did anything surprise you?

00:01:38.793 --> 00:01:40.210
AUDIENCE: The tiny, tiny screws.

00:01:40.210 --> 00:01:42.650
KRISTEN RAILEY:
Tiny, tiny screws?

00:01:42.650 --> 00:01:46.222
How about that
corner in the back?

00:01:46.222 --> 00:01:49.485
AUDIENCE: [INAUDIBLE]
compared to the whole camera.

00:01:49.485 --> 00:01:51.360
KRISTEN RAILEY: Compared
to the whole camera?

00:01:51.360 --> 00:01:51.860
Great.

00:01:51.860 --> 00:01:55.720
Anything else, different from
the screws, surprise you guys?

00:01:55.720 --> 00:01:58.520
Did anyone have a film camera?

00:01:58.520 --> 00:02:00.700
Was there anything
unique about that?

00:02:00.700 --> 00:02:02.520
Had you ever used one before?

00:02:02.520 --> 00:02:05.686
AUDIENCE: Yeah, the
reusable film cameras, I

00:02:05.686 --> 00:02:07.827
was surprised at how
simple they were.

00:02:07.827 --> 00:02:08.910
KRISTEN RAILEY: Oh, great.

00:02:08.910 --> 00:02:12.340
So you guys are going to learn
a lot about cameras today

00:02:12.340 --> 00:02:15.080
and you're even going
to build one yourself.

00:02:15.080 --> 00:02:19.000
But before we get
started, I would

00:02:19.000 --> 00:02:21.860
like to know, who
comes to mind when

00:02:21.860 --> 00:02:25.480
you think of an engineer
or a computer scientist?

00:02:25.480 --> 00:02:29.340
So close your eyes
and think about what

00:02:29.340 --> 00:02:34.550
this person looks like and what
they do during their day job.

00:02:34.550 --> 00:02:37.314
Think about it for a minute.

00:02:37.314 --> 00:02:38.180
All right.

00:02:38.180 --> 00:02:40.800
Open your eyes.

00:02:40.800 --> 00:02:43.566
What do you guys think?

00:02:43.566 --> 00:02:44.482
AUDIENCE: [INAUDIBLE].

00:02:47.314 --> 00:02:51.090
No, they sit at a
computer [INAUDIBLE].

00:02:51.090 --> 00:02:52.520
KRISTEN RAILEY: Nice.

00:02:52.520 --> 00:02:55.410
How about you in
the green jacket.

00:02:55.410 --> 00:02:58.020
What do you think?

00:02:58.020 --> 00:02:59.910
AUDIENCE: Coding and--

00:02:59.910 --> 00:03:02.042
KRISTEN RAILEY: What do
they do with the code?

00:03:02.042 --> 00:03:04.930
AUDIENCE: Make programs.

00:03:04.930 --> 00:03:08.070
KRISTEN RAILEY: Anyone
think of hacking?

00:03:08.070 --> 00:03:09.170
Or Mark Zuckerberg?

00:03:09.170 --> 00:03:12.060
You guys have probably
seen him before, right?

00:03:12.060 --> 00:03:17.250
So those are all
really good examples

00:03:17.250 --> 00:03:20.830
of what computer programming is.

00:03:20.830 --> 00:03:22.716
Have you guys seen
Iron Man before?

00:03:22.716 --> 00:03:23.554
AUDIENCE: Yeah.

00:03:23.554 --> 00:03:25.220
KRISTEN RAILEY: So
he does a combination

00:03:25.220 --> 00:03:27.670
of computer science and
mechanical engineering,

00:03:27.670 --> 00:03:31.200
but engineering is a lot
more broad than that.

00:03:31.200 --> 00:03:33.930
And in just camera
technology alone,

00:03:33.930 --> 00:03:36.500
here's some interesting examples
that you might not normally

00:03:36.500 --> 00:03:38.010
think about.

00:03:38.010 --> 00:03:41.610
Have you guys heard of
unmanned aerial vehicles?

00:03:41.610 --> 00:03:42.540
Drones?

00:03:42.540 --> 00:03:45.300
So a big thing is
putting cameras on them.

00:03:45.300 --> 00:03:48.410
And they can do things like
go inside a burning building

00:03:48.410 --> 00:03:50.870
and map it out for a fireman.

00:03:50.870 --> 00:03:53.500
Another example is
high-speed photography

00:03:53.500 --> 00:03:56.220
for understanding
how animals work

00:03:56.220 --> 00:03:59.020
or satellite imaging
for figuring out

00:03:59.020 --> 00:04:00.710
how healthy the earth is.

00:04:00.710 --> 00:04:02.840
And finally, Instagram,
which you guys

00:04:02.840 --> 00:04:04.490
have probably heard of.

00:04:04.490 --> 00:04:08.470
And like I said before,
camera technology is not just

00:04:08.470 --> 00:04:09.680
about computer science.

00:04:09.680 --> 00:04:12.632
It involves materials
and mechanics and optics.

00:04:12.632 --> 00:04:14.340
And you guys are going
to learn about all

00:04:14.340 --> 00:04:18.380
those different components
during this workshop.

00:04:18.380 --> 00:04:22.230
So that leads me to what
the goal of today is.

00:04:22.230 --> 00:04:25.950
You are going to discover
what camera technology is,

00:04:25.950 --> 00:04:28.900
how to apply it, and
gain engineering skills.

00:04:28.900 --> 00:04:32.810
And you're going to do this by
building a camera, programming

00:04:32.810 --> 00:04:35.260
your own Instagram
filters, and also

00:04:35.260 --> 00:04:37.670
learn about applications
of camera technology.

00:04:37.670 --> 00:04:40.170
We have an awesome
lineup of speakers,

00:04:40.170 --> 00:04:44.740
including space cameras
and medical imaging.

00:04:44.740 --> 00:04:47.210
And we have a bunch of
cool demos at the end,

00:04:47.210 --> 00:04:50.230
including high-speed
photography, infrared,

00:04:50.230 --> 00:04:51.071
and 3D scanning.

00:04:53.660 --> 00:04:56.580
So this is just an
overview of today.

00:04:56.580 --> 00:05:00.340
We'll begin with a keynote
speaker, Chris Clark,

00:05:00.340 --> 00:05:02.190
who I'll introduce in a minute.

00:05:02.190 --> 00:05:05.270
And you're going to spend the
first half of the day building

00:05:05.270 --> 00:05:06.080
a camera.

00:05:06.080 --> 00:05:08.130
You're going to start
off with an introduction

00:05:08.130 --> 00:05:10.310
to all the different
components of a camera.

00:05:10.310 --> 00:05:12.330
And then you're going
to get your hands on all

00:05:12.330 --> 00:05:14.930
the different components, and
assemble them, and program

00:05:14.930 --> 00:05:17.500
it to take a picture when
you press the button.

00:05:17.500 --> 00:05:21.190
And then during
lunch, we have a panel

00:05:21.190 --> 00:05:23.070
from the Society
of Women Engineers,

00:05:23.070 --> 00:05:25.710
and the Women's
Technology Program at MIT,

00:05:25.710 --> 00:05:28.000
and an all-girls robotics team.

00:05:28.000 --> 00:05:30.880
So if you're interested in
a certain part of today,

00:05:30.880 --> 00:05:32.560
like, you want to do
more coding or you

00:05:32.560 --> 00:05:35.000
want to do more building,
you should pay attention

00:05:35.000 --> 00:05:35.890
to these programs.

00:05:35.890 --> 00:05:37.960
So they'll talk about what
you can do for longer.

00:05:37.960 --> 00:05:41.370
They have summer programs and
programs throughout the year.

00:05:41.370 --> 00:05:43.950
And then in the second
half of the day,

00:05:43.950 --> 00:05:46.160
we're going to do
image processing.

00:05:46.160 --> 00:05:47.830
So you're going to
get an introduction

00:05:47.830 --> 00:05:51.390
to how a computer
understands what an image is,

00:05:51.390 --> 00:05:53.710
and how you can
manipulate it with math,

00:05:53.710 --> 00:05:56.620
and make those really
cool Instagram filters.

00:05:56.620 --> 00:06:00.050
And after you program
your own, we'll

00:06:00.050 --> 00:06:02.680
have a conclusion and talk
about what you learned.

00:06:02.680 --> 00:06:05.350
And finally, we have
those really cool demos

00:06:05.350 --> 00:06:08.650
that I mentioned before, where
you'll rotate around and check

00:06:08.650 --> 00:06:10.930
them out.

00:06:10.930 --> 00:06:17.430
So you all received a picture
of a famous female engineer.

00:06:17.430 --> 00:06:22.790
If you didn't get one, I have
a bunch of extras for you guys.

00:06:22.790 --> 00:06:26.870
So if you didn't get one, I'm
going to start in the front

00:06:26.870 --> 00:06:31.130
and just pass them
down, and take one, OK?

00:06:31.130 --> 00:06:34.360
So you guys are going to
be in teams of four today,

00:06:34.360 --> 00:06:37.440
and those famous female
engineers and computer

00:06:37.440 --> 00:06:39.590
scientists are your teams.

00:06:39.590 --> 00:06:44.030
So you're going to work together
on all these challenges,

00:06:44.030 --> 00:06:46.608
and the volunteers
will work with you

00:06:46.608 --> 00:06:47.607
during those activities.

00:06:50.740 --> 00:06:52.800
Now we have some
really boring details,

00:06:52.800 --> 00:06:54.830
but they are very important.

00:06:54.830 --> 00:06:59.970
So in case of an emergency,
there is stairs in the back.

00:06:59.970 --> 00:07:03.620
So when you exit, turn left,
and go all the way to the back,

00:07:03.620 --> 00:07:08.900
and then you cluster
outside in the quad area.

00:07:08.900 --> 00:07:11.120
And there are bathrooms.

00:07:11.120 --> 00:07:13.852
When you go outside, turn
left, and the bathroom

00:07:13.852 --> 00:07:14.560
is on your right.

00:07:14.560 --> 00:07:16.351
And the volunteers know
where they are too,

00:07:16.351 --> 00:07:18.880
if you have any questions.

00:07:18.880 --> 00:07:22.010
And you guys are probably
interested in this, where you

00:07:22.010 --> 00:07:23.720
can find the photos afterwards.

00:07:23.720 --> 00:07:27.940
So I highly encourage you to
take as many photos as possible

00:07:27.940 --> 00:07:31.150
with your cameras that
you build and they

00:07:31.150 --> 00:07:34.150
will be posted on our
Facebook page afterwards.

00:07:34.150 --> 00:07:36.380
It's called Girls Who Build.

00:07:36.380 --> 00:07:39.990
And finally, all the
links to the activities

00:07:39.990 --> 00:07:45.030
that you do today, and the
code that you worked on,

00:07:45.030 --> 00:07:48.210
and the resources,
like, if you want

00:07:48.210 --> 00:07:50.300
to build your own
camera at home,

00:07:50.300 --> 00:07:52.740
we'll send that to you too.

